1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,582
Every morning, you forget a movie you just watched.

2
00:00:03,582 --> 00:00:08,761
Dreams can feel more real than real life, yet they vanish within minutes of waking.

3
00:00:08,761 --> 00:00:11,631
Why does the brain delete its own masterpiece?

4
00:00:11,631 --> 00:00:14,938
The answer is stranger than 'dreams are meaningless.'

5
00:00:15,538 --> 00:00:19,758
First, a myth: you don't forget dreams because they're unimportant.

6
00:00:19,758 --> 00:00:25,363
You forget them because your brain's memory chemistry literally changes when you wake up.

7
00:00:25,363 --> 00:00:31,347
During REM sleep, the chemicals that write memories into long-term storage are at their lowest.

8
00:00:31,347 --> 00:00:34,812
The recording button is off while the movie is playing.

9
00:00:34,812 --> 00:00:39,788
So most dreams were never stored in the first place. There's nothing to recall.

10
00:00:40,388 --> 00:00:42,656
Then why do we remember some dreams?

11
00:00:42,656 --> 00:00:45,807
Usually because you woke up in the middle of them.

12
00:00:45,807 --> 00:00:50,471
Wake up during REM, and the chemistry flips on. The last dream gets saved.

13
00:00:50,471 --> 00:00:55,639
That's why the dreams you remember are almost always the ones right before waking.

14
00:00:55,639 --> 00:01:01,563
It's also why stress and alarms make dream recall jump. Any interruption becomes a save point.

15
00:01:02,163 --> 00:01:05,288
Some scientists think the forgetting is the point.

16
00:01:05,288 --> 00:01:12,288
Dreams are raw simulation, full of noise. Storing everything would blur the line between memory and imagination.

17
00:01:12,288 --> 00:01:15,475
The brain keeps the lesson and deletes the footage.

18
00:01:15,475 --> 00:01:20,038
People who remember every dream often struggle to tell memory from dream.

19
00:01:20,638 --> 00:01:23,625
So tomorrow morning, don't chase the whole dream.

20
00:01:23,625 --> 00:01:26,673
It was never a recording. It was a live broadcast.

21
00:01:26,673 --> 00:01:32,098
Write one image down before you move, and you'll keep the only frame that was ever yours.

22
00:01:32,098 --> 00:01:36,975
And maybe that's the real question: how much of tonight will you decide to keep?
